# StreamerShield local dev environment — managed by direnv on top of flake.nix.
# Enable once with:  direnv allow
#
# This used to run `python3 -m venv` + `pip install` against whatever python3 the
# host happened to have, which meant `direnv allow` never entered the nix shell:
# the flake, its pinned python 3.12 and every dev-<verb> wrapper were invisible to
# exactly the workflow this repo's docs push hardest. `use flake` delegates to
# flake.nix instead, so direnv and `nix develop` are the same environment.
#
# It gives you the toolchain, not a .venv: run `dev-setup` once to create ./.venv
# from requirements-dev.txt (gitignored; delete it to force a clean rebuild), then
# `dev-test` / `dev-run`, which use that interpreter by absolute path — no
# `activate` needed. `dev-help` lists the full command map.
#
# Install nix-direnv if the reload is slow; it caches the shell between entries.
use flake

# Load local, gitignored config/secrets if present (TWITCH_*, DB_*, URLs, flags).
# Kept here rather than in the flake's shellHook: a store-built shell must not
# depend on a gitignored file, and config.py load_dotenv()s this same .env anyway.
dotenv_if_exists .env

# NOTE: TensorFlow is intentionally NOT part of the local dev dependency set —
# requirements-dev.txt leaves it out and the model trainer and prediction API run
# in Docker (python:3.12-slim); see Dockerfile.api and requirements-api.txt. The
# flake's python 3.12 pin does have wheels for it, so `dev-setup -r
# requirements-api.txt` adds it to the same .venv when you really need it locally
# (~600 MB).
